@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/belief#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<> a orexis:OntologyGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is

#  OREXIS BELIEF.
#  The belief package's own words: beliefs, and the beliefs derived on top of them. A graph is
#  written as any belief is, and the rules the store holds conclude what follows from it — its
#  revisions — into a graph of its own, derived from it. Any belief is accepted, and revised;
#  nothing refuses one and nothing stands between a writer and the store. A validation and a
#  proposal state to serve it were built here and struck: belief revision keeps the new
#  information, dropping testimony over a shape is a gate wearing revision's name, and a graph
#  with no catalogue row is already invisible to every reader, so a writer that means its graph
#  never to be seen without its revisions writes the graph, revises it and classifies it last.
#  The rules are SHACL 1.2 Inference Rules (W3C FPWD, 2026-09-17), adopted as they stand and
#  nothing added: a graph in the role of a rules graph is typed sh:RulesGraph, a package's rules
#  are a sh:RuleSet with sh:hasRule and sh:includesRuleSet, a rule is a sh:SPARQLRule with
#  sh:construct, placed by sh:layer and sh:order, taken out by sh:deactivated, run once by
#  sh:runOnce; `revise` runs a rules graph's default rule set — every global rule in it, one not
#  linked to a shape by sh:rule — layer by layer to a fixpoint, rules of one order not seeing each
#  other's inferences within an iteration, and keeps the inferences in an inference graph apart
#  from the base graph, never deleting. The draft names that graph as a role and not a class, so
#  the one word here is the class of it. The engine is the store's own, not SHACL's. Classes only:
#  which graph is an agent's is genesis's to create and the catalogue's to say.
#
#  ONE AXIOM ON THE DRAFT'S TERM, AND NO WORD: a rules graph is a graph. A boot reads a document
#  only where the vocabulary puts its kind beneath `orexis:Graph` — a kind says who reads the
#  document (a-documents-kind-says-who-reads-it) — and this package is the reader of rules graphs,
#  so it is the one to say so. Without it every package's and domain's rules were passed over.

sh:RulesGraph rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph .

:RevisionGraph a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:BeliefGraph ;
    rdfs:label "Revision graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """The revisions of ONE source graph — the beliefs the rules conclude of it, and
    nothing the source itself says; SHACL 1.2's inference graph, for one source. prov:wasDerivedFrom
    the source, orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Derived, the source's owner and period copied where its
    row states them. Apart from the source because a conclusion is a function of its premises —
    two sources with the same facts have the same conclusions, so a world is hashed by its own
    facts and never by these — and because deletion is never a rule's: the graph is dropped
    whole and rebuilt when the source is rewritten, and goes when the source goes. A reader
    hands it to a rule beside its source; nothing forks it, nothing hashes it, nothing deletes
    from it.""" .

:settled a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :RevisionGraph ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "settled" ;
    rdfs:comment """Whether the rules reached a fixpoint over this graph's source. `revise` spends a
    BUDGET of rule executions per call, the unit revision spends, and a source the budget cuts
    short keeps what was concluded with this false on its row; the deliberator continues it on
    the next pass, and at boot re-queues every row that says false, so a cut survives a restart
    and a rule set that never settles is reported every pass rather than looped on.""" .

:budget a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Self ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "budget" ;
    rdfs:comment """How many rule executions one pass of revision may spend, across every source it
    revises; a source the budget cuts short is continued by the next pass. A stance: the agent
    states it of itself in its self graph and the deliberator reads it when it is made; where it
    states none, two hundred and fifty-six. One source's share of a pass stays `revise`'s own
    constant, which says how a pass's budget is spent and not how much.""" .

#  THE ROLE THIS PACKAGE SERVES (knowledge/domain/belief/deliberator.md). An agent declared a
#  deliberator — in its self graph, or by a role a domain puts beneath this one — runs the
#  deliberator, which revises what it believes by the rules it holds; the boot loads this package
#  for it and for nothing else. It needs nothing of the world: an agent holding no rules graph may
#  still be declared one, and revises nothing.

:Deliberator a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Deliberator" ;
    rdfs:comment """An agent that revises what it believes by the rules it holds: the role whose
    part is the deliberator, one concept with it. Declared, never derived; a role beneath this
    one — the observer's, a market host's — loads this package with its own.""" .
